Troubleshooting
If ansible-playbook reports Unable to find role 'webserver', investigate how Ansible resolves role paths: run ansible-config dump | grep ROLES_PATH, check your current working directory when invoking the playbook, and compare the location of roles/webserver against the paths Ansible actually searches. What does this tell you about how relative role paths are resolved?

If the template task fails with an error such as AnsibleUndefinedVariable: 'ansible_default_ipv4' is undefined, investigate whether gather_facts is enabled for the play and whether the module you are using populates that fact on your target OS. Run ansible <host> -i inventory -m setup -a "filter=ansible_default_ipv4" to inspect what facts are actually available, then determine whether the fix is a missing gather_facts: yes, a typo in the variable name, or a network interface that does not report an IPv4 address in the way you expect.

Conclusion
In this lab, you built a complete Ansible role from the ground up, moving through all seven standard role directories (tasks, handlers, templates, files, vars, defaults, and meta) and wiring them together into a working webserver role that installs Apache, configures the firewall, deploys a Jinja2-templated index page, and verifies its own deployment. You then combined that custom role with a community role installed via ansible-galaxy and a requirements.yml file, demonstrating how production Ansible projects mix in-house and third-party automation. Finally, you validated the role with an assertion-based test playbook and documented it with a README and an ansible-lint pass, reflecting the practices used to keep roles reusable and maintainable in real automation pipelines. With these skills, you are ready to package roles into Ansible Collections, publish them to a private or public Galaxy namespace, and incorporate automated testing tools such as Molecule to further harden your roles before using them in production environments.
